"""English navigation introductions adapted to the reviewed VI groups and existing EN sections."""
INTRO = {
('Aesthetic/Style','internet-alt-aesthetics'): 'Follow the reference that interests you rather than collecting every recognizable detail. Dark clothing, club imagery and different visions of the future can overlap in color without sharing the same background. Compare silhouette, surface and the role of each accent in the individual guides before deciding what to keep in an everyday outfit.',
('Aesthetic/Style','j-fashion-gyaru'): 'Begin with the clothing you want to try, then separate its shape, finishing and cultural context. Anime-inspired outfits do not establish a particular J-Fashion branch. Himekaji, Ryousangata and Tenshi Kaiwai each need their own references, while Wafuku concerns a clothing system with particular construction and uses rather than another interchangeable aesthetic label.',
('Aesthetic/Style','global-style-knowledge'): 'Familiar tops, trousers and shoes can support very different finishes. Compare Basic, Workwear and Stockholm Style for the structure you want, then explore Edgy, Rock Chic or Hipster references separately. Pink palettes, layered visual detail and runway design answer other questions. The burnout article is broader reading: its wardrobe example offers a small everyday convenience, without assigning clothing a therapeutic role. Vedette describes a performance role rather than an outfit aesthetic.',
('Aesthetic/Style','retro-historical'): 'Read a specific period before borrowing its outline. Waist placement and length help distinguish a 1920s reference; Victorian garments need attention to their actual construction rather than lace alone. Photographs of 1990s Vietnam and modern Victorian Goth interpretations also show why historical material and present-day outfit styling should remain separate questions.',
('Ao','Choose a Guide by Garment'): 'Start with the neckline, sleeve and hem you need. Tanks and singlets raise coverage questions; Henleys, polos and blouses change the opening around the face. Cardigans and coats need trying over the intended base, including the shoulders and lining. For printed anime or jersey tops, assess the graphic on the actual cut. A product name cannot settle those fit decisions or establish protective performance.',
('Quan','Choose a Guide by Garment'): 'Identify the line from thigh to hem before deciding on the top. Tapered and curved legs place volume differently, while skinny, slim and Bermuda cuts have their own length and movement checks. Read pockets, rise, lining and fabric together for cargo, workout and sweatpants options. Dungarees add an upper section; striped trousers and double denim bring another surface question once the shape is settled.',
('Vay-dam','Choose a Guide by Garment'): 'Choose the construction before treating color or print as the whole decision. Pencil skirts need seated and walking room; tiers and pleats distribute fabric differently. Floral, polka-dot and plain dresses can be compared with the same accessories. For eveningwear, Qipao, fringe or a long pinafore, test the neckline, fastening and movement on the actual design rather than transferring one formula between unrelated shapes.',
('Giay','Choose Footwear Around Four Outfit Variables'): 'Separate the intended activity from the visual finish. Walking, running, gym and wet-weather footwear need product-specific checks; a familiar outline does not make one pair a replacement for every use. With heels, Oxford shoes, mules or platforms, try the chosen garment hem and the actual route. Color and customization come after the construction, fit and role in the outfit are clear.',
('Phu-kien','Choose a Specific Accessory Guide'): 'Give each accessory a job before repeating a color or metal. Earrings, scarves and ties sit near the face and neckline; rings and bracelets need checking during hand movements. Bags add carrying capacity as well as a shape at the hip. Berets, fedoras and caps create different outlines around the head, so try them with the hair, glasses and outer layer you normally use.',
('Vai','cac-ten-thuong-gap'): 'Read the fiber label alongside the construction, surface and finished garment. Wool names, spandex content, linen and suede do not replace the care instructions for the product. Poplin, Oxford, canvas and khaki need assessment of actual thickness and cut; flannel, tweed and chiffon add other surface questions. Umi and kate silk require product details, while tie-dye describes a color treatment rather than a complete material specification.',
('Vai','doc-ten-vai'): 'Keep fiber and construction as separate pieces of information. Polyester describes a fiber, while knitting describes how fabric is formed. Neither name alone establishes the thickness, fit or care of a finished top. Use these two explanations to frame the questions before comparing the examples further down the page.',
('Kinh','xem-mat'): 'Compare one feature at a time rather than assigning a compulsory frame to a face category. Oval, square and cat-eye shapes change lens height, corners and the upper line. Keep the hairstyle and viewing distance similar, then judge the actual width and wearing comfort alongside the visual effect you want.',
('Kinh','thuc-hanh-thu'): 'Material, rim and lens outline answer different questions. Titanium still needs a product-specific fit check; round lenses may be quiet or prominent depending on diameter and rim thickness. Rimless and semi-rimless frames change how the edge is visible, without removing the need to assess the bridge, temples and whole outline.',
('Outfit-core','How to Use the Item Guide'): 'Move from the garment you have to an outfit you can use. Definitions, moodboards and outfit photographs help record choices; a capsule plan tests how pieces combine repeatedly. Proportion guides offer options rather than instructions to hide a body. For a purchase, establish the garment role, budget and measurements before interpreting store terms such as auth, local brand, boutique or sold out.',
}
